// Point tone curve (Curves v2, slice 2) — the user's own freely-placed tone
// curve, a SEPARATE, ADDITIVE stage that runs immediately after the
// parametric curve in ToneCurve.cikernel next door.
//
// The sixth custom Core Image kernel here, after SelectiveColor, ColorRange,
// the two Dehaze kernels and ToneCurve.
//
// WHY THIS KERNEL IS MONOTONE WHILE ToneCurve.cikernel BESIDE IT IS NATURAL
// CUBIC — the single most likely thing to confuse a future reader, so it is
// recorded in both files:
//   - ToneCurve.cikernel reproduces CIToneCurve's undocumented spline
//     byte-exactly, which is what keeps every already-saved sidecar and
//     preset rendering identically. That spline is a NATURAL cubic; slice
//     1's pre-work measured a monotone fit at 0.016 error against it, an
//     order of magnitude past the suite's 0.002 tolerance. Monotone cannot
//     be used there.
//   - This kernel's control points are placed FREELY by the user, and a
//     natural cubic through freely-placed points overshoots: the tone
//     response can visibly reverse between two points, which reads as a
//     rendering bug rather than as a curve. Fritsch-Carlson cannot.
// Two interpolants, two genuinely different requirements.
//
// Reference: F. N. Fritsch and R. E. Carlson, "Monotone Piecewise Cubic
// Interpolation", SIAM J. Numer. Anal. 17(2), 1980, 238-246. The tangent
// solve and its monotonicity filter run once per edit on the CPU, in
// MonotoneToneCurve.swift — a colour kernel cannot and should not redo it
// per pixel. This file only EVALUATES the Hermite segments.
//
// DOMAIN: as with ToneCurve.cikernel, the values arriving here are ALREADY
// perceptual (applyPerceptualAdjustments' encodeToPerceptual/decodeToLinear
// sandwich), and this kernel encodes a SECOND time internally so its control
// points share the parametric curve's domain exactly. Do not "simplify" that
// away, and do not try to hoist a shared encode across the two curve stages
// — that would change the parametric layer's arithmetic and break its
// byte-exactness.
//
// OUT-OF-DOMAIN, DELIBERATELY ASYMMETRIC, matching ToneCurve.cikernel:
//   u > last x  -> LINEAR extrapolation along the curve's own end tangent,
//                  so highlight headroom survives an active curve.
//   u < first x -> CLAMPED to its y. The leftmost point is a black point;
//                  extrapolating there would drive values negative.
//
// ARGUMENT LAYOUT — must match MonotoneToneCurve.paddedForKernel() and
// EditPipeline.applyPointCurve exactly:
//   xa = x0 x1 x2 x3      xb = x4 x5 x6 x7
//   ya = y0 y1 y2 y3      yb = y4 y5 y6 y7
//   ma = m0 m1 m2 m3      mb = m4 m5 m6 m7     (Hermite tangents)
//   channelMask = (r, g, b), each > 0.5 or not — WHICH channels this pass
//                 touches (Curves v2 slice 3). This is the SAME kernel used
//                 for both the composite curve (mask (1,1,1), byte-identical
//                 to the pre-slice-3 unconditional call) and up to three
//                 more single-channel passes (mask (1,0,0)/(0,1,0)/(0,0,1))
//                 — see EditPipeline.applyPointCurve for why up to 4 passes
//                 of ONE kernel, not four kernels or one kernel carrying
//                 four curves' worth of uniforms (argument-budget reasons,
//                 recorded there).
//
// A MASK, NOT A NUMERIC SELECTOR, and a TERNARY, not mix(). mix(a, b, 1.0)
// is not guaranteed bit-exact to b in IEEE-754; the ternary is exact in
// both directions, which matters because the composite curve's render has
// a standing byte-exactness requirement (PointToneCurveKernelTests'
// accuracy: 0 gate). A ternary also SKIPS pointCurveValue's pow() calls
// for an untouched channel rather than computing and discarding them.
// Branching on a uniform mask is the cheap kind (every lane takes the same
// branch) and matches existing precedent — SelectiveColor.cikernel and
// ColorRange.cikernel both branch; this codebase's actual rule (see the
// unrolled-segments note below) is no LOOPS, not no branches.
//
// THE PAD-TO-8 SCHEME, which is why there is no point-count argument and no
// separate extrapolation branch below. Swift solves the real N points
// (2 <= N <= 8) and then appends 8-N synthetic points that continue along
// the end tangent AND carry that tangent. Because those points are collinear
// with the end tangent, the Hermite collinearity identity (h00 + h01 == 1,
// h01 + h10 + h11 == t, see MonotoneToneCurve.hermiteSegment) makes every
// padded segment evaluate to EXACTLY the straight line the extrapolation
// rule would have produced. So padding is provably output-identical rather
// than approximate, and the kernel is always exactly 7 segments with
// mb.w always the end slope. The pad step is arbitrary; nothing depends on
// its value.
//
// Segments are UNROLLED, not looped. No kernel in this codebase uses a loop,
// and "a constant-bound for compiles and renders correctly in CI Kernel
// Language here" is exactly the kind of claim this project's conventions
// forbid asserting without running it. With pad-to-8 the segment count is a
// compile-time constant anyway, so unrolling costs nothing.

float srgbEncode(float x) {
    float a = abs(x);
    float e = (a <= 0.0031308) ? 12.92 * a : 1.055 * pow(a, 1.0 / 2.4) - 0.055;
    return (x < 0.0) ? -e : e;
}

float srgbDecode(float x) {
    float a = abs(x);
    float d = (a <= 0.04045) ? a / 12.92 : pow((a + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4);
    return (x < 0.0) ? -d : d;
}

// One cubic Hermite segment. Twin of MonotoneToneCurve.hermiteSegment.
float hermiteSegment(float u, float x0, float x1, float y0, float y1, float m0, float m1) {
    float h = x1 - x0;
    float t = (u - x0) / h;
    float t2 = t * t;
    float t3 = t2 * t;
    float h00 =  2.0 * t3 - 3.0 * t2 + 1.0;
    float h10 =        t3 - 2.0 * t2 + t;
    float h01 = -2.0 * t3 + 3.0 * t2;
    float h11 =        t3 -       t2;
    return h00 * y0 + h10 * h * m0 + h01 * y1 + h11 * h * m1;
}

float pointCurveValue(float v, vec4 xa, vec4 xb, vec4 ya, vec4 yb, vec4 ma, vec4 mb) {
    float u = srgbEncode(v);
    float y;
    if (u <= xa.x) {
        // Below the first control point — clamp, do not extrapolate.
        y = ya.x;
    } else if (u >= xb.w) {
        // Above the last (padded) point. With pad-to-8 this is the same
        // straight line the padded segments already produce, continued.
        y = yb.w + mb.w * (u - xb.w);
    } else if (u < xa.y) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xa.x, xa.y, ya.x, ya.y, ma.x, ma.y);
    } else if (u < xa.z) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xa.y, xa.z, ya.y, ya.z, ma.y, ma.z);
    } else if (u < xa.w) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xa.z, xa.w, ya.z, ya.w, ma.z, ma.w);
    } else if (u < xb.x) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xa.w, xb.x, ya.w, yb.x, ma.w, mb.x);
    } else if (u < xb.y) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xb.x, xb.y, yb.x, yb.y, mb.x, mb.y);
    } else if (u < xb.z) {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xb.y, xb.z, yb.y, yb.z, mb.y, mb.z);
    } else {
        y = hermiteSegment(u, xb.z, xb.w, yb.z, yb.w, mb.z, mb.w);
    }
    return srgbDecode(y);
}

kernel vec4 pointToneCurve(__sample s, vec4 xa, vec4 xb, vec4 ya, vec4 yb, vec4 ma, vec4 mb, vec3 channelMask) {
    // Un-premultiply, same as ToneCurve.cikernel — a tone curve applies to
    // UNPREMULTIPLIED colour, measured there against CIToneCurve (with alpha
    // 0.5 its result read back at exactly 0.5x the opaque result).
    float a = s.a;
    vec3 rgb = (a > 0.0) ? s.rgb / a : s.rgb;

    vec3 outRGB = vec3(
        (channelMask.r > 0.5) ? pointCurveValue(rgb.r, xa, xb, ya, yb, ma, mb) : rgb.r,
        (channelMask.g > 0.5) ? pointCurveValue(rgb.g, xa, xb, ya, yb, ma, mb) : rgb.g,
        (channelMask.b > 0.5) ? pointCurveValue(rgb.b, xa, xb, ya, yb, ma, mb) : rgb.b
    );

    // Repremultiply; alpha untouched — a tone curve must not alter coverage.
    return vec4(outRGB * a, a);
}
